3\mid2^{a-2} - 5k_{\text{odd}} &&\text{ (from the first identity)} \\
\end{align*}

Now, let's analyze all the multiples of $3$ that are divisible by $5$: $3$, $12$, ..., $15$, $42$, ... That list of multiples divisible by $5$ corresponds to the even powers of $3$. The smallest such power that adds up to $2^a$ would be $2^{a-2}$. We learned earlier that the smallest factors of $k$ which would make $k357k$ divisible by $18$ include some multiples of $5$ for the $5$ factors in $5^2$. Write $a=b+2$, where $b\geqslant0$, as our expression has a $3^a$ term on top of the $3^b$ term resulting from the $x$:

\begin{align*}
