To solve the inequality, we can cross-multipiply and find the integer intervals that x can be in:

\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{3} < \frac{x}{5}, \\
\frac{6}{7} < \frac{x}{5},
\end{align*}
Multiply each side by $5$,
\begin{align*}
5 \left(\frac{2}{3}\right) < x & => x > \frac{20}{3}, \\
5 \left(\frac{6}{7}\right) < x & => x > \frac{30}{7}.
\end{align*}
Since the smallest value x is greater than $\frac{20}{3}$, x will certainly be greater than $\frac{30}{7}$, as $\frac{30}{7}$ is smaller than $\frac{20}{3}$.
So, the smallest value x can be greater than $\frac{30}{7}$.

Now, we find the smallest possible integer value greater than $\frac{30}{7}$. To do this, we find the next integer above $\frac{30}{7}$.

\begin{align*}
\frac{30}{7}\text{ rounded up } = \boxed{4}.
\end{align*}
Since we are told that x is an integer, and 4 is the smallest positive integer in our solution interval, we see that

\boxed{4}
is the answer to the problem in its simplest exact form.

