First, find the radius $r$ of the tube from its lateral surface area $A$ with using the formula for the lateral surface area of a cylinder: $2\pi rh + 2\pi r^2$.
In this case, $A = 48\pi$ and $h = 6 \text{ cm}$.
\begin{align*}
48\pi &= 2\cdot \pi(6) + 2\cdot (\pi r)^2 \\
&= 12\pi + 2\pi r^2 \\
r^2 &= \frac{48\pi - 12\pi}{2\pi} = 24\pi \\
r &= \sqrt{24\pi} = 4.90 \approx 4.9 \text{ cm}
\end{align*}
Now that we have the radius, use the formula for the volume of a cylinder: $V = \pi r^2 h$ to find the volume $V$ in cubic centimeters.
\begin{align*}
V &= \pi r^2 h = \pi (4.9)^2 (6) \\
V &= 83.04 \approx 83 \text{ cubic centimeters}
\end{align*}

\boxed{ 83 \text{ cubic centimeters } }

