Let the side length of the cube, $a$ meters.

Since the volume of a cube is given by $V = a^3$, we can write:
\begin{align*}
0.027~\text{m}^3 &= a^3 \\
a^3 &= 0.027~\text{m}^3 \\
a &= \sqrt[3]{0.027~\text{m}^3} \\
\intertext{The surface area of a cube is $A = 6a^2$. Writing in terms of $a$,}
A &= 6a^2 \\
A &= 6 \cdot \left( \sqrt[3]{0.027~\text{m}^3} \right)^2 \\
A &= 6 \cdot \sqrt[3]{\left( \sqrt[3]{0.027~\text{m}^3} \right)^{\strut 3}} \\
\intertext{Since $\sqrt[3]{0.027~\text{m}^3}$ is the side length of the cube,}
A &= 6 \cdot \sqrt[3]{\left( a \right)^{\strut 3}} \\
A &= 6 \cdot a \\
\end{align*}

\boxed{ 6\sqrt[3]{0.027} \text{ m}^2 }

\end{document}
```

