The formula for the volume of a cube is:

\text{ Volume }= \text{ edge length }^3

A 2-cm-by-2cm-by-2cm cube has an initial volume of \boxed{ 8 \text{ cubic cm } }

If the volume is increased by 19 cubic centimeters:

\text{ New Volume } = \text{ initial volume } + 19
\text{ New Volume } = 8\text{ cubic cm } + 19\text{ cubic cm } = 27\text{ cubic cm }

Let's denote the side length of the new cube as X. The new volume can be represented as:

\text{ New Volume } = \text{ edge length(new) }^3 = \boxed{ X^3 = 27 }

By solving this equation, we find:

\boxed{ X \boxed{ = \text{ cube root of }27 } } = \boxed{ X = 3\sqrt[3]{3} \text{ cm } }
